A||||10||Richard Parnell||Stock jack can't handle V8 weight||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||08-10-2001||03:03 PM||||I recently tried using the stock jack that comes with the Fiero.  It was hard to raise the car but it went up high enough to take the rear wheel off.  The problem was when I began to lower the car the jack just collapsed down to flat.  I'm glad it didn't decide to do that while I had my head stuck up under the car tightening the front bolts where the 350 bolts to the cradle.  Does anyone know of a good strong (and small) jack that I can get to replace this one? I recently moved and sold all my large shop type jacks so I want something that will still fit up front where the stock jack goes.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||08-10-2001||03:37 PM||||I use a cheap, light duty (think WalMart) floor jack. <BR>Maybe just a bit larger than you want, but still light enough to carry with one hand. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||08-10-2001||04:26 PM||||v8s add what, 400lbs (highball esitmate) tops?   I can 't imagine the fiero's jack was engineered with so poor a safety margin.<P>More likely that yours failed due to damage or defects.  <P>But in any event, a much stronger jack would be a welcome change ;)  (v8 or no)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||KRMFiero||08-10-2001||04:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>v8s add what, 400lbs (highball esitmate) tops</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe a extra 100 lbs, i doubt more then 150 extra lbs. Jack is probley just so old and hasnt been used in a while or something...||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||08-10-2001||06:13 PM||||A telescoping jack handles more weight than a scissor jack anyday and you can find them fairly small that will do the trick compared to those floor jacks that are nice, but a little bulky. Anyway that's my 2 cents. That's all I can afford my car is still in the shop after two weeks for a clutch and exhuast manifold leak. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>Can you say, "CHING! CHING! $!!!$!!$??$??$?"<P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||EdsB52||08-10-2001||06:37 PM||||Fieros come with two types of jacks you know. The older style scissor and the newer type with the cool built-it handle. I don't know what the weight handling capacities are. I agree w/a previous poster who said the weight should be negligible.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Richard Parnell||08-10-2001||06:41 PM||||Thanks for the input.  Actually the engine adds about 150 extra pounds.  I agree it shouldn't have failed, the jack looks like new but always was harder than hell to wind up so who knows what happened.  It did give me a chill when it collapsed.  Next time I'll be sure and put blocks under the car.  I was in a hurry and was lucky I think.||||reg||1
Z||000007||BlackDragon||08-10-2001||08:56 PM||||try an aluminum block    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
